const GENMATH = [{"id": "gen:algebra-01", "topic": "algebra-expressions", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>If 9<sup><i>x</i></sup> &times; 27<sup><i>x</i> &minus; 1</sup> = 3<sup><i>x</i> + 5</sup>, what is the value of <i>x</i>?</p>", "opts": ["0.5", "1.25", "1.5", "2"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>Write every base as a power of 3: 9<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 3<sup>2<i>x</i></sup> and 27<sup><i>x</i> &minus; 1</sup> = 3<sup>3(<i>x</i> &minus; 1)</sup> = 3<sup>3<i>x</i> &minus; 3</sup>. Multiplying powers of the same base adds exponents, so the left side is 3<sup>5<i>x</i> &minus; 3</sup>. Setting exponents equal: 5<i>x</i> &minus; 3 = <i>x</i> + 5, so 4<i>x</i> = 8 and <i>x</i> = 2. Check: 81 &times; 27 = 2187 = 3<sup>7</sup>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This comes from a sign error, 5<i>x</i> + 3 = <i>x</i> + 5, which gives <i>x</i> = 0.5.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This ignores the &minus;1 in the exponent of 27 and solves 5<i>x</i> = <i>x</i> + 5.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This adds 3 to the exponent instead of multiplying by 3, writing 27<sup><i>x</i> &minus; 1</sup> as 3<sup><i>x</i> + 2</sup>, which gives 3<i>x</i> + 2 = <i>x</i> + 5.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> 5<i>x</i> &minus; 3 = <i>x</i> + 5 gives <i>x</i> = 2.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-02", "topic": "algebra-expressions", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>The value of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap\">(4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> &times; (5 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) &divide; (2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>)</span> can be written in scientific notation as <i>a</i> &times; 10<sup><i>n</i></sup>, where 1 &le; <i>a</i> &lt; 10 and <i>n</i> is an integer. What is the value of <i>a</i> + <i>n</i>?</p>", "a": "8", "exp": "<p>First square: (4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 16 &times; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup>. Multiply by 5 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>: 16 &times; 5 = 80 and 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> &times; 10<sup>7</sup> = 10<sup>1</sup>, giving 80 &times; 10<sup>1</sup>. Divide by 2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>: 80 &divide; 2 = 40 and 10<sup>1</sup> &divide; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> = 10<sup>3</sup>, giving 40 &times; 10<sup>3</sup>. In scientific notation that is 4 &times; 10<sup>4</sup>, so <i>a</i> = 4, <i>n</i> = 4, and <i>a</i> + <i>n</i> = 8. (Common slips: forgetting to square the 4, or adding the exponent &minus;2 instead of subtracting it, which gives 4 &times; 10<sup>0</sup>.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-03", "topic": "algebra-expressions", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>For <i>x</i> &ne; 0 and <i>x</i> &ne; 2, which expression is equivalent to</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i></span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\"><i>x</i></span></span> divided by <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\"><i>x</i></span></span> ?</p>", "opts": ["<i>x</i> &minus; 2", "<i>x</i> + 2", "<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 4", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i> + 2</span><span class=\"fden\">2<i>x</i></span></span>"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Put each part over the common denominator 2<i>x</i>. The numerator is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 4</span><span class=\"fden\">2<i>x</i></span></span> and the denominator is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i> &minus; 2</span><span class=\"fden\">2<i>x</i></span></span>. Dividing fractions with the same denominator cancels the 2<i>x</i>, leaving <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 4</span><span class=\"fden\"><i>x</i> &minus; 2</span></span>. Factor <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 4 = (<i>x</i> + 2)(<i>x</i> &minus; 2) and cancel <i>x</i> &minus; 2 (allowed because <i>x</i> &ne; 2) to get <i>x</i> + 2. Check with <i>x</i> = 4: (2 &minus; 0.5) &divide; (0.5 &minus; 0.25) = 1.5 &divide; 0.25 = 6 = 4 + 2.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This is a sign slip when factoring and canceling; it fails the check at <i>x</i> = 4, where it gives 2 instead of 6.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The expression simplifies to <i>x</i> + 2.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This stops after combining the numerator and forgets to divide by <i>x</i> &minus; 2.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This divides by 2<i>x</i> twice instead of letting the 2<i>x</i> in numerator and denominator cancel.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-04", "topic": "equations-inequalities", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>How many integers <i>x</i> satisfy both of the inequalities below?</p><p style=\"text-align:center\">&minus;3(<i>x</i> &minus; 4) &ge; 2<i>x</i> &minus; 13 and 5 &minus; 2<i>x</i> &lt; 11</p>", "opts": ["3", "7", "8", "9"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>First inequality: &minus;3<i>x</i> + 12 &ge; 2<i>x</i> &minus; 13, so 25 &ge; 5<i>x</i> and <i>x</i> &le; 5. Second: &minus;2<i>x</i> &lt; 6; dividing by &minus;2 flips the sign, so <i>x</i> &gt; &minus;3. The integers from &minus;2 through 5 are &minus;2, &minus;1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, which is 8 integers.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This distributes &minus;3 as &minus;3<i>x</i> &minus; 12, giving <i>x</i> &le; 0 and only the 3 integers &minus;2, &minus;1, 0.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This counts 5 &minus; (&minus;2) = 7, forgetting that a list from &minus;2 to 5 has one more integer than the difference.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> &minus;3 &lt; <i>x</i> &le; 5 contains 8 integers.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This includes <i>x</i> = &minus;3, but the second inequality is strict: 5 &minus; 2(&minus;3) = 11 is not less than 11.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-05", "topic": "equations-inequalities", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Nia has 38 coins, all dimes and quarters. If she had the same number of coins but the number of dimes and the number of quarters were switched, she would have 90 cents more than she has now. How many quarters does Nia have?</p>", "opts": ["16", "19", "22", "32"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>Let <i>d</i> be the number of dimes and <i>q</i> the number of quarters, so <i>d</i> + <i>q</i> = 38. Now she has 10<i>d</i> + 25<i>q</i> cents; after switching she would have 10<i>q</i> + 25<i>d</i>. The difference is (10<i>q</i> + 25<i>d</i>) &minus; (10<i>d</i> + 25<i>q</i>) = 15<i>d</i> &minus; 15<i>q</i> = 90, so <i>d</i> &minus; <i>q</i> = 6. Adding to <i>d</i> + <i>q</i> = 38 gives 2<i>d</i> = 44, <i>d</i> = 22, and <i>q</i> = 16. Check: 220 + 400 = 620 cents now; 160 + 550 = 710 cents switched; 710 &minus; 620 = 90.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> <i>q</i> = 16.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This assumes equal numbers of each coin, ignoring the 90-cent condition.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This is the number of dimes, or the result of a sign error (<i>q</i> &minus; <i>d</i> = 6) that swaps the two answers.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This subtracts the difference 6 from 38 instead of solving the system.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-06", "topic": "equations-inequalities", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>What value of <i>x</i> satisfies the equation below? Express your answer as a decimal.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i> &minus; 1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2<i>x</i> + 5</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>x</i></span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> &minus; 2</p>", "a": "1.25", "exp": "<p>Multiply every term by 12, the least common denominator: 4(<i>x</i> &minus; 1) &minus; 3(2<i>x</i> + 5) = 2<i>x</i> &minus; 24. Distribute carefully, including the minus sign on the second term: 4<i>x</i> &minus; 4 &minus; 6<i>x</i> &minus; 15 = 2<i>x</i> &minus; 24, so &minus;2<i>x</i> &minus; 19 = 2<i>x</i> &minus; 24. Then 5 = 4<i>x</i> and <i>x</i> = 1.25. Check: 0.25 &divide; 3 = 0.08333&hellip;, 7.5 &divide; 4 = 1.875, left side = &minus;1.79166&hellip;; right side = 0.20833&hellip; &minus; 2 = &minus;1.79166&hellip;. (Forgetting to distribute the minus to the +5 gives &minus;2<i>x</i> + 11 = 2<i>x</i> &minus; 24, the wrong answer 8.75.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-07", "topic": "functions-patterns", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>In a sequence of positive integers, each term after the second is the sum of the two terms before it. The 4th term is 11 and the 7th term is 47. What is the 1st term?</p>", "opts": ["2", "3", "4", "7"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Let the first two terms be <i>a</i> and <i>b</i>. The sequence is <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>a</i> + <i>b</i>, <i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i>, 2<i>a</i> + 3<i>b</i>, 3<i>a</i> + 5<i>b</i>, 5<i>a</i> + 8<i>b</i>. So <i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i> = 11 and 5<i>a</i> + 8<i>b</i> = 47. From the first, <i>a</i> = 11 &minus; 2<i>b</i>; substituting, 55 &minus; 10<i>b</i> + 8<i>b</i> = 47, so <i>b</i> = 4 and <i>a</i> = 3. The sequence is 3, 4, 7, 11, 18, 29, 47.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Guessing 2 as the first term forces the second term to be 4.5, not an integer.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The first term is 3.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This is the 2nd term, <i>b</i>, not the 1st.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This is the 3rd term, found by stopping at <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = 7.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-08", "topic": "functions-patterns", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>A function <i>f</i> satisfies <i>f</i>(<i>n</i> + 1) = 2<i>f</i>(<i>n</i>) &minus; 3 for every positive integer <i>n</i>. If <i>f</i>(3) = 11, what is the value of <i>f</i>(1)?</p>", "a": "5", "exp": "<p>Work backward. With <i>n</i> = 2: <i>f</i>(3) = 2<i>f</i>(2) &minus; 3, so 11 = 2<i>f</i>(2) &minus; 3 and <i>f</i>(2) = 7. With <i>n</i> = 1: <i>f</i>(2) = 2<i>f</i>(1) &minus; 3, so 7 = 2<i>f</i>(1) &minus; 3 and <i>f</i>(1) = 5. Check forward: 2(5) &minus; 3 = 7 and 2(7) &minus; 3 = 11. (Applying the rule forward from 11 instead, 2(11) &minus; 3 = 19, gives <i>f</i>(4), not <i>f</i>(1).)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-09", "topic": "functions-patterns", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A linear function <i>f</i> satisfies <i>f</i>(2) = 7 and <i>f</i>(5) = 1. For what value of <i>x</i> is <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i>?</p>", "opts": ["<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">7</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The slope is (1 &minus; 7) &divide; (5 &minus; 2) = &minus;2, so <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = &minus;2<i>x</i> + <i>c</i>. Using <i>f</i>(2) = 7: &minus;4 + <i>c</i> = 7, so <i>c</i> = 11 and <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 11 &minus; 2<i>x</i>. Set 11 &minus; 2<i>x</i> = 2<i>x</i>: 11 = 4<i>x</i>, so <i>x</i> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span>. Check: <i>f</i>(2.75) = 11 &minus; 5.5 = 5.5 = 2(2.75).</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> <i>x</i> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This solves <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i> instead of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This is the midpoint of the inputs 2 and 5; the question does not ask where <i>f</i> takes its average value.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This solves <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 0, finding where the function crosses the <i>x</i>-axis.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:algebra-10", "topic": "algebra-expressions", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>If 2<sup><i>a</i></sup> = 5 and 2<sup><i>b</i></sup> = 3, what is the value of <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">8<sup><i>a</i></sup></span><span class=\"fden\">4<sup><i>b</i></sup></span></span>?</p>", "opts": ["<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">125</span><span class=\"fden\">9</span></span>", "116", "1125"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Rewrite with base 2: 8<sup><i>a</i></sup> = (2<sup>3</sup>)<sup><i>a</i></sup> = (2<sup><i>a</i></sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 5<sup>3</sup> = 125, and 4<sup><i>b</i></sup> = (2<sup><i>b</i></sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 3<sup>2</sup> = 9. The quotient is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">125</span><span class=\"fden\">9</span></span>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This multiplies 5 by 3 and 3 by 2 instead of raising to the 3rd and 2nd powers.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 8<sup><i>a</i></sup> &divide; 4<sup><i>b</i></sup> = 125 &divide; 9.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This subtracts 125 &minus; 9; dividing powers subtracts exponents, not values.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This multiplies 125 &times; 9 instead of dividing.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-01", "topic": "geometry-angles", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>In triangle <i>ABC</i>, the measure of angle <i>B</i> is twice the measure of angle <i>A</i>, and the measure of angle <i>C</i> is 20&deg; more than the measure of angle <i>B</i>. The bisector of angle <i>C</i> meets side <i>AB</i> at point <i>D</i>. What is the measure of angle <i>ADC</i>?</p>", "opts": ["42&deg;", "74&deg;", "98&deg;", "106&deg;"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>Let angle <i>A</i> = <i>x</i>. Then angle <i>B</i> = 2<i>x</i> and angle <i>C</i> = 2<i>x</i> + 20. The angles sum to 180&deg;: 5<i>x</i> + 20 = 180, so <i>x</i> = 32. The angles are 32&deg;, 64&deg;, and 84&deg;. The bisector splits angle <i>C</i> into two 42&deg; angles. In triangle <i>ADC</i>, angle <i>ADC</i> = 180&deg; &minus; 32&deg; &minus; 42&deg; = 106&deg;.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 42&deg; is half of angle <i>C</i>, not the angle at <i>D</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 74&deg; is angle <i>BDC</i>, the other angle at <i>D</i>, found by using angle <i>B</i> instead of angle <i>A</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 98&deg; comes from solving 5<i>x</i> = 180 (dropping the extra 20&deg;), which gives <i>x</i> = 36.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> 180&deg; &minus; 32&deg; &minus; 42&deg; = 106&deg;.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-02", "topic": "geometry-angles", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>In triangle <i>PQR</i>, <i>PQ</i> = <i>PR</i> and the measure of angle <i>QPR</i> is 100&deg;. Point <i>S</i> lies on side <i>QR</i> so that <i>PS</i> = <i>QS</i>. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle <i>SPR</i>?</p>", "a": "60", "exp": "<p>Since <i>PQ</i> = <i>PR</i>, the base angles are equal: angle <i>Q</i> = angle <i>R</i> = (180&deg; &minus; 100&deg;) &divide; 2 = 40&deg;. Since <i>PS</i> = <i>QS</i>, triangle <i>PQS</i> is also isosceles, so angle <i>SPQ</i> = angle <i>SQP</i> = 40&deg;. Angle <i>SPQ</i> is part of the 100&deg; angle at <i>P</i>, so angle <i>SPR</i> = 100&deg; &minus; 40&deg; = 60&deg;. (Check: triangle <i>PSR</i> then has angles 60&deg;, 40&deg;, and 80&deg;, and angle <i>PSR</i> = 80&deg; is supplementary to angle <i>PSQ</i> = 100&deg;.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-03", "topic": "geometry-angles", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>In trapezoid <i>ABCD</i>, side <i>AB</i> is parallel to side <i>DC</i>. The diagonals <i>AC</i> and <i>BD</i> intersect at point <i>E</i>. The measure of angle <i>BAC</i> is 35&deg; and the measure of angle <i>BDC</i> is 50&deg;. What is the measure of angle <i>AED</i>?</p>", "opts": ["15&deg;", "85&deg;", "95&deg;", "130&deg;"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Because <i>AB</i> &#8741; <i>DC</i> and <i>BD</i> is a transversal, angle <i>ABD</i> and angle <i>BDC</i> are alternate interior angles, so angle <i>ABD</i> = 50&deg;. In triangle <i>ABE</i>, angle <i>BAE</i> = 35&deg; and angle <i>ABE</i> = 50&deg;, so angle <i>AEB</i> = 180&deg; &minus; 35&deg; &minus; 50&deg; = 95&deg;. Angles <i>AEB</i> and <i>AED</i> form a straight line along <i>BD</i>, so angle <i>AED</i> = 180&deg; &minus; 95&deg; = 85&deg;. (Shortcut: angle <i>AED</i> is an exterior angle of triangle <i>ABE</i>, so it equals 35&deg; + 50&deg;.)</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 15&deg; is the difference 50&deg; &minus; 35&deg;; the exterior angle is the sum, not the difference.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 35&deg; + 50&deg; = 85&deg;.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 95&deg; is angle <i>AEB</i>; the question asks for its supplement, angle <i>AED</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 130&deg; is 180&deg; &minus; 50&deg;, which ignores the 35&deg; angle entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-04", "topic": "geometry-measure", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A circle is inscribed in a square of area 64, so the circle touches all four sides of the square. A second, smaller square is inscribed in the circle, so all four of its vertices lie on the circle. What is the area of the region that is inside the circle but outside the smaller square?</p>", "opts": ["16&pi; &minus; 16", "64 &minus; 16&pi;", "16&pi; &minus; 32", "64&pi; &minus; 32"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The large square has side 8, so the circle has diameter 8 and radius 4, and its area is &pi;(4)<sup>2</sup> = 16&pi;. The smaller square has the circle's diameter as its diagonal, so its diagonal is 8. A square with diagonal <i>d</i> has area <i>d</i><sup>2</sup> &divide; 2 (it is two congruent right triangles with legs 8 and half the other diagonal, or use side = 8 &divide; &radic;2), so its area is 64 &divide; 2 = 32. The region inside the circle but outside the small square has area 16&pi; &minus; 32.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 16&pi; &minus; 16 uses side 4 for the small square; 4 is the radius, not the side.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 64 &minus; 16&pi; is the region inside the large square but outside the circle.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 16&pi; &minus; 32.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 64&pi; &minus; 32 uses the diameter 8 as the radius of the circle.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-05", "topic": "geometry-measure", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>An isosceles trapezoid has parallel sides of length 10 centimeters and 22 centimeters, and each of its two equal legs is 10 centimeters long. What is the area of the trapezoid, in square centimeters?</p>", "opts": ["96", "128", "160", "256"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Drop a perpendicular from each end of the shorter base to the longer base. The two right triangles on the ends together account for 22 &minus; 10 = 12 centimeters, so each has a horizontal leg of 6. Each leg of the trapezoid is the hypotenuse, 10, so the height is &radic;(10<sup>2</sup> &minus; 6<sup>2</sup>) = &radic;64 = 8 (a 6-8-10 triangle). Area = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>(10 + 22)(8) = 16 &times; 8 = 128.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 96 uses 6, the horizontal leg of the end triangle, as the height.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 16 &times; 8 = 128.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 160 uses the slanted leg, 10, as the height.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 256 = (10 + 22)(8) forgets to halve the sum of the bases.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-06", "topic": "geometry-measure", "rating": 5, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>A solid wooden cube has edges of length 6 centimeters. A square hole, 2 centimeters by 2 centimeters, is cut straight through the cube from the center of one face to the center of the opposite face, so the hole's sides are parallel to the cube's edges. What is the total surface area, in square centimeters, of the resulting solid, including the inside walls of the hole?</p>", "a": "256", "exp": "<p>The original cube has surface area 6 &times; 6<sup>2</sup> = 216. The hole removes a 2 &times; 2 = 4 square from each of two opposite faces: 216 &minus; 8 = 208. It also adds four inside walls, each a rectangle 2 centimeters wide and 6 centimeters long (the full depth of the cube): 4 &times; 12 = 48. Total: 208 + 48 = 256.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-07", "topic": "geometry-measure", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A circle has radius 12. A sector of this circle (a pie-shaped piece bounded by two radii and an arc) has an arc of length 4&pi;. What is the area of the sector?</p>", "opts": ["4&pi;", "12&pi;", "24&pi;", "48&pi;"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The circumference is 2&pi;(12) = 24&pi;, so the arc is 4&pi; &divide; 24&pi; = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> of the circle. The sector is the same fraction of the circle's area: <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> &times; &pi;(12)<sup>2</sup> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> &times; 144&pi; = 24&pi;.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 4&pi; is the arc length, not the area.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 12&pi; comes from using 12&pi; (half the circumference) as the full circumference and 72&pi; as the area, or simply halving the correct answer.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> One sixth of 144&pi; is 24&pi;.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 48&pi; comes from comparing 4&pi; to 12&pi; (the radius times &pi;) instead of to the full circumference 24&pi;, giving <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> instead of <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span>.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-08", "topic": "coordinate-geometry", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>The vertices of a triangle are (1, 2), (7, 2), and (4, <i>k</i>), where <i>k</i> &gt; 2. The area of the triangle is 15 square units. What is the perimeter of the triangle?</p>", "opts": ["6 + 2&radic;34", "16", "6 + &radic;34", "6 + 2&radic;58"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The side from (1, 2) to (7, 2) is horizontal with length 6. Using it as the base, the height is the vertical distance from (4, <i>k</i>) to the line <i>y</i> = 2, which is <i>k</i> &minus; 2. Then <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>(6)(<i>k</i> &minus; 2) = 15, so <i>k</i> &minus; 2 = 5 and <i>k</i> = 7. The third vertex (4, 7) is directly above the midpoint of the base, so the two slanted sides are equal. Each one goes 3 units across and 5 units up: length &radic;(3<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup>) = &radic;34. Perimeter = 6 + 2&radic;34.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> 6 + &radic;34 + &radic;34.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 16 = 6 + 5 + 5 uses the height 5 as the length of each slanted side.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 6 + &radic;34 counts only one of the two slanted sides.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 6 + 2&radic;58 uses <i>k</i> = 7 as the vertical rise instead of <i>k</i> &minus; 2 = 5.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-09", "topic": "coordinate-geometry", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Point <i>A</i> has coordinates (&minus;4, 3). Point <i>B</i> is the reflection of <i>A</i> across the line <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>, and point <i>C</i> is the reflection of <i>A</i> across the <i>y</i>-axis. What is the area of triangle <i>ABC</i>, in square units?</p>", "opts": ["14", "24", "28", "56"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Reflecting across <i>y</i> = <i>x</i> swaps the coordinates: <i>B</i> = (3, &minus;4). Reflecting across the <i>y</i>-axis changes the sign of <i>x</i>: <i>C</i> = (4, 3). Points <i>A</i> and <i>C</i> both have <i>y</i> = 3, so <i>AC</i> is horizontal with length 4 &minus; (&minus;4) = 8. The height from <i>B</i> to the line <i>y</i> = 3 is 3 &minus; (&minus;4) = 7. Area = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>(8)(7) = 28.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 14 halves the product twice.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 24 comes from reflecting <i>A</i> across the <i>x</i>-axis instead of the line <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>, which gives <i>B</i> = (&minus;4, &minus;3) and legs 6 and 8.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> &times; 8 &times; 7 = 28.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 56 = 8 &times; 7 forgets the <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> in the triangle area formula.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:geometry-10", "topic": "coordinate-geometry", "rating": 5, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Point <i>A</i> has coordinates (1, 2) and point <i>B</i> has coordinates (7, 10). A point <i>P</i> on the <i>x</i>-axis is the same distance from <i>A</i> as it is from <i>B</i>. What is the <i>x</i>-coordinate of <i>P</i>?</p>", "a": "12", "exp": "<p>Let <i>P</i> = (<i>k</i>, 0). Set the squared distances equal: (<i>k</i> &minus; 1)<sup>2</sup> + (0 &minus; 2)<sup>2</sup> = (<i>k</i> &minus; 7)<sup>2</sup> + (0 &minus; 10)<sup>2</sup>. Expanding: <i>k</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 2<i>k</i> + 1 + 4 = <i>k</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 14<i>k</i> + 49 + 100. The <i>k</i><sup>2</sup> terms cancel, leaving &minus;2<i>k</i> + 5 = &minus;14<i>k</i> + 149, so 12<i>k</i> = 144 and <i>k</i> = 12. Check: from (12, 0) to (1, 2) is &radic;(121 + 4) = &radic;125, and to (7, 10) is &radic;(25 + 100) = &radic;125.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-01", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>When the positive integer <i>N</i> is divided by 7, the remainder is 3. When <i>N</i> is divided by 5, the remainder is 4. What is the remainder when 2<i>N</i> is divided by 35?</p>", "opts": ["7", "13", "14", "24"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Numbers with remainder 3 when divided by 7: 3, 10, 17, 24, 31, &hellip; Of these, 24 is the first with remainder 4 when divided by 5, so <i>N</i> = 24 + 35<i>k</i> for some whole number <i>k</i>. Then 2<i>N</i> = 48 + 70<i>k</i>, and 70<i>k</i> is a multiple of 35, so the remainder of 2<i>N</i> divided by 35 is the remainder of 48 divided by 35, which is 13.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Adds the two remainders 3 + 4 without finding <i>N</i>.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Since <i>N</i> leaves remainder 24 when divided by 35, 2<i>N</i> leaves the same remainder as 48, which is 48 &minus; 35 = 13.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Doubles the sum of the remainders, 2(3 + 4).</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Stops at the remainder of <i>N</i> divided by 35 and forgets to double.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-02", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>A two-digit positive integer is equal to 7 times the sum of its digits. What is the sum of all two-digit positive integers with this property?</p>", "a": "210", "exp": "<p>Let the tens digit be <i>a</i> and the units digit be <i>b</i>, so the number is 10<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>. Then 10<i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = 7(<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>) gives 3<i>a</i> = 6<i>b</i>, so <i>a</i> = 2<i>b</i>. Since <i>a</i> is a nonzero digit, <i>b</i> can be 1, 2, 3, or 4, giving the numbers 21, 42, 63, and 84. (Check: 21 = 7 &times; 3, 84 = 7 &times; 12.) Their sum is 21 + 42 + 63 + 84 = 210.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-03", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>What is the units digit of 7<sup>2026</sup> + 3<sup>2026</sup>?</p>", "opts": ["0", "2", "8", "9"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The units digits of powers of 7 repeat in the cycle 7, 9, 3, 1 (length 4), and the units digits of powers of 3 repeat in the cycle 3, 9, 7, 1. Since 2026 = 4 &times; 506 + 2, the exponent 2026 lands on the second position of each cycle: 7<sup>2026</sup> ends in 9 and 3<sup>2026</sup> ends in 9. The sum ends in the units digit of 9 + 9 = 18, which is 8.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Uses the third position of each cycle (3 + 7 = 10), as if the remainder of 2026 divided by 4 were 3.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Uses the last position of each cycle (1 + 1), as if 2026 were a multiple of 4.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 7<sup>2026</sup> ends in 9 and 3<sup>2026</sup> ends in 9, and 9 + 9 = 18 ends in 8.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Finds the units digit of one of the two terms and stops.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-04", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>The sum of four consecutive even integers is 12 less than 3 times the greatest of the integers. What is the least of the four integers?</p>", "opts": ["&minus;6", "&minus;4", "0", "6"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>Let the integers be <i>n</i>, <i>n</i> + 2, <i>n</i> + 4, and <i>n</i> + 6. Their sum is 4<i>n</i> + 12, and 12 less than 3 times the greatest is 3(<i>n</i> + 6) &minus; 12 = 3<i>n</i> + 6. So 4<i>n</i> + 12 = 3<i>n</i> + 6, giving <i>n</i> = &minus;6. The integers are &minus;6, &minus;4, &minus;2, 0: their sum is &minus;12, and 3 &times; 0 &minus; 12 = &minus;12.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The integers are &minus;6, &minus;4, &minus;2, and 0, and the least is &minus;6.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This is the second integer, not the least.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This is the greatest of the four integers, not the least.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sign error when solving 4<i>n</i> + 12 = 3<i>n</i> + 6 (subtracting in the wrong direction gives <i>n</i> = 6).</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-05", "topic": "arithmetic", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>What is the value of &minus;3<sup>2</sup> &minus; (&minus;2)<sup>3</sup> &divide; (&minus;4) + |5 &minus; 9| &times; (&minus;1)?</p>", "opts": ["&minus;15", "&minus;11", "&minus;7", "3"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>Work each piece first. &minus;3<sup>2</sup> means &minus;(3<sup>2</sup>) = &minus;9. (&minus;2)<sup>3</sup> = &minus;8, and (&minus;8) &divide; (&minus;4) = 2. |5 &minus; 9| = |&minus;4| = 4, and 4 &times; (&minus;1) = &minus;4. Then &minus;9 &minus; 2 + (&minus;4) = &minus;15.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> &minus;9 &minus; 2 &minus; 4 = &minus;15.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Gets (&minus;8) &divide; (&minus;4) = &minus;2 instead of 2, giving &minus;9 + 2 &minus; 4.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Treats |5 &minus; 9| as &minus;4, so the last term becomes +4 instead of &minus;4.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Reads &minus;3<sup>2</sup> as (&minus;3)<sup>2</sup> = 9, giving 9 &minus; 2 &minus; 4.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-06", "topic": "arithmetic", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>How many integers <i>x</i> satisfy both |2<i>x</i> &minus; 7| &le; 11 and |<i>x</i> + 3| &gt; 2?</p>", "a": "10", "exp": "<p>The first inequality means &minus;11 &le; 2<i>x</i> &minus; 7 &le; 11, so &minus;4 &le; 2<i>x</i> &le; 18 and &minus;2 &le; <i>x</i> &le; 9. The second means <i>x</i> + 3 &gt; 2 or <i>x</i> + 3 &lt; &minus;2, so <i>x</i> &gt; &minus;1 or <i>x</i> &lt; &minus;5. No integer with <i>x</i> &lt; &minus;5 is in the range &minus;2 to 9, so only <i>x</i> &gt; &minus;1 matters: the integers 0, 1, 2, &hellip;, 9. That is 10 integers. (The integers &minus;2 and &minus;1 satisfy the first inequality but not the second.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-07", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>In the five-digit integer 34<i>A</i>5<i>B</i>, the letters <i>A</i> and <i>B</i> each stand for a single digit. If the integer is divisible by 36, what is the greatest possible value of <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>?</p>", "opts": ["6", "9", "15", "18"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>A number is divisible by 36 when it is divisible by both 4 and 9. Divisible by 4: the number formed by the last two digits, 5<i>B</i>, must be a multiple of 4, so <i>B</i> = 2 (52) or <i>B</i> = 6 (56). Divisible by 9: the digit sum 3 + 4 + <i>A</i> + 5 + <i>B</i> = 12 + <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> must be a multiple of 9, so 12 + <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> is 18 or 27 (it cannot reach 36), giving <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> = 6 or 15. With <i>B</i> = 6, <i>A</i> = 9 gives <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> = 15, and 34,956 = 36 &times; 971. So the greatest possible value is 15.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Stops at the first solution (<i>A</i> = 4, <i>B</i> = 2 or <i>A</i> = 0, <i>B</i> = 6) and misses the larger digit sum.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This is the greatest possible value of <i>A</i> alone, not of <i>A</i> + <i>B</i>.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> <i>B</i> must be 2 or 6, and 12 + <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> must be a multiple of 9; <i>A</i> = 9, <i>B</i> = 6 works and gives 15.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Assumes both digits can be 9, but 34,959 is not divisible by 4.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-08", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>When the positive integer <i>n</i> is divided by 9, the remainder is 7. What is the remainder when <i>n</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>n</i> is divided by 9?</p>", "opts": ["0", "4", "5", "7"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>Write <i>n</i> = 9<i>k</i> + 7. Every term of <i>n</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>n</i> that contains a factor of 9<i>k</i> is a multiple of 9, so the remainder is the same as the remainder of 7<sup>2</sup> + 2(7) = 49 + 14 = 63 when divided by 9. Since 63 = 9 &times; 7, the remainder is 0. (Check with <i>n</i> = 7: 49 + 14 = 63.)</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> 63 is a multiple of 9, so the remainder is 0.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This is the remainder of <i>n</i><sup>2</sup> alone (49 = 45 + 4); the 2<i>n</i> term was dropped.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This is the remainder of 2<i>n</i> alone (14 = 9 + 5); the <i>n</i><sup>2</sup> term was dropped.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Assumes the remainder stays 7 no matter what is done to <i>n</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-09", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>The product of three consecutive positive integers is 21 times their sum. What is the greatest of the three integers?</p>", "a": "9", "exp": "<p>Let the integers be <i>n</i>, <i>n</i> + 1, and <i>n</i> + 2. Their sum is 3(<i>n</i> + 1), so the condition is <i>n</i>(<i>n</i> + 1)(<i>n</i> + 2) = 21 &times; 3(<i>n</i> + 1) = 63(<i>n</i> + 1). Dividing both sides by <i>n</i> + 1 gives <i>n</i>(<i>n</i> + 2) = 63, and 7 &times; 9 = 63, so <i>n</i> = 7. The integers are 7, 8, 9: their product is 504, their sum is 24, and 21 &times; 24 = 504. The greatest is 9.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:numtheory-10", "topic": "number-theory", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>The digits of a three-digit positive integer have a sum of 12, and its tens digit is twice its units digit. When the digits are written in reverse order, the resulting three-digit integer is 396 less than the original. What is the original integer?</p>", "opts": ["246", "543", "624", "642"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>Let the digits be <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>c</i> (hundreds, tens, units). The original integer is 100<i>a</i> + 10<i>b</i> + <i>c</i> and the reversed one is 100<i>c</i> + 10<i>b</i> + <i>a</i>; their difference is 99(<i>a</i> &minus; <i>c</i>) = 396, so <i>a</i> &minus; <i>c</i> = 4. With <i>b</i> = 2<i>c</i> and <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> + <i>c</i> = 12: (<i>c</i> + 4) + 2<i>c</i> + <i>c</i> = 12, so 4<i>c</i> = 8 and <i>c</i> = 2, <i>b</i> = 4, <i>a</i> = 6. The integer is 642, and 642 &minus; 246 = 396.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This is the reversed integer, not the original.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Uses <i>a</i> &minus; <i>c</i> = 2 (dividing 396 by 198 instead of 99); 543 &minus; 345 = 198, not 396.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Swaps the tens and units digits: here the units digit is twice the tens digit.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> <i>a</i> &minus; <i>c</i> = 4, <i>b</i> = 2<i>c</i>, and the digit sum 12 give 642; 642 &minus; 246 = 396.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-01", "topic": "probability-counting", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A bag contains 4 red marbles, 3 blue marbles, and 5 green marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one at a time without replacement. What is the probability that the two marbles are different colors?</p>", "opts": ["<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">19</span><span class=\"fden\">66</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">47</span><span class=\"fden\">132</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">47</span><span class=\"fden\">66</span></span>", "<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">47</span><span class=\"fden\">72</span></span>"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Use the complement. There are 12 &times; 11 = 132 ordered ways to draw two marbles. Both the same color: red&ndash;red 4 &times; 3 = 12, blue&ndash;blue 3 &times; 2 = 6, green&ndash;green 5 &times; 4 = 20, total 38. So P(same) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">38</span><span class=\"fden\">132</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">19</span><span class=\"fden\">66</span></span>, and P(different) = 1 &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">19</span><span class=\"fden\">66</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">47</span><span class=\"fden\">66</span></span>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This is the probability that both marbles are the same color; the question asks for different colors.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Counting the color pairs red&ndash;blue, red&ndash;green, blue&ndash;green only once each (4&middot;3 + 4&middot;5 + 3&middot;5 = 47) over 132 forgets that each pair can be drawn in either order.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 1 &minus; 19/66 = 47/66.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This uses 12 &times; 12 = 144 outcomes, as if the first marble were put back before the second draw.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-02", "topic": "probability-counting", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A jar contains 10 marbles, each either red or blue. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. The probability that both marbles are red is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">15</span></span>. How many red marbles are in the jar?</p>", "opts": ["3", "4", "6", "12"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Let <i>r</i> be the number of red marbles. P(both red) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>r</i></span><span class=\"fden\">10</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>r</i> &minus; 1</span><span class=\"fden\">9</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>r</i>(<i>r</i> &minus; 1)</span><span class=\"fden\">90</span></span>. Setting this equal to <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">15</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">12</span><span class=\"fden\">90</span></span> gives <i>r</i>(<i>r</i> &minus; 1) = 12, so <i>r</i> = 4 (since 4 &times; 3 = 12).</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 3 &times; 2 = 6, not 12; this comes from solving <i>r</i>(<i>r</i> &minus; 1) = 12 as <i>r</i> &minus; 1 = 4 and then reporting the wrong one of the two consecutive numbers.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 4/10 &times; 3/9 = 12/90 = 2/15.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 6 is the number of blue marbles, not red.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 12 is the product <i>r</i>(<i>r</i> &minus; 1), not <i>r</i> itself.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-03", "topic": "probability-counting", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Ana, Ben, Cal, Dee, and Eli will sit in a row of 5 seats. Ana must sit in one of the two end seats, and Ben and Cal must not sit next to each other. How many different seating arrangements are possible?</p>", "a": "24", "exp": "<p>Place Ana first: 2 choices (left end or right end). The other four people fill the remaining 4 seats, which form a row of 4. Without restriction that is 4! = 24 ways. Count the ways Ben and Cal <i>are</i> adjacent in a row of 4 seats: 3 adjacent seat pairs &times; 2 orders (Ben&ndash;Cal or Cal&ndash;Ben) &times; 2! ways to seat Dee and Eli = 12. So Ben and Cal are apart in 24 &minus; 12 = 12 ways. Total: 2 &times; 12 = 24.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-04", "topic": "statistics", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A list of five positive integers has a mean of 10, a median of 12, and a single mode of 12. What is the greatest possible value of the largest integer in the list?</p>", "opts": ["22", "23", "24", "25"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The sum is 5 &times; 10 = 50. Write the list in order as <i>a</i> &le; <i>b</i> &le; 12 &le; <i>d</i> &le; <i>e</i>. Since 12 is the only mode, 12 must appear at least twice, so <i>d</i> = 12 (using a third 12 would only shrink <i>e</i>). To make <i>e</i> as large as possible, make <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> as small as possible, but they cannot be equal or there would be a second mode. So <i>a</i> = 1, <i>b</i> = 2, and <i>e</i> = 50 &minus; 1 &minus; 2 &minus; 12 &minus; 12 = 23. The list 1, 2, 12, 12, 23 checks.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 1, 2, 12, 13, 22 has the right mean and median, but 12 is not its mode.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 1, 2, 12, 12, 23.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 1, 1, 12, 12, 24 has two modes, 1 and 12, so 12 is not the single mode.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 0, 1, 12, 12, 25 uses 0, which is not a positive integer.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-05", "topic": "statistics", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Hana&rsquo;s mean score on her first 5 tests was 82. After her 6th test, her mean score rose to 85. She wants her mean score after 7 tests to be 87. What score does she need on the 7th test?</p>", "opts": ["94", "97", "99", "100"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Work with totals. After 6 tests the total is 6 &times; 85 = 510. A mean of 87 on 7 tests needs a total of 7 &times; 87 = 609. So the 7th score must be 609 &minus; 510 = 99. (Shortcut: raising the mean of the 6 earlier tests by 2 each costs 12 points above the new mean, 87 + 12 = 99.)</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 82 + 2 &times; 6 = 94 starts from the old mean of 82 instead of the target 87.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 87 + 2 &times; 5 = 97 raises only 5 earlier tests by 2 points; there are 6 earlier tests.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 609 &minus; 510 = 99.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 100 is her score on the 6th test (510 &minus; 410), not what she needs on the 7th.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-06", "topic": "statistics", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Twenty-five students were asked how many siblings they have. Four students have 0 siblings, nine students have 1 sibling, and every other student has either 2 or 3 siblings. If the mean number of siblings is exactly 1.6, how many students have 3 siblings?</p>", "opts": ["5", "6", "7", "12"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let <i>x</i> students have 2 siblings and <i>y</i> have 3. Then <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 25 &minus; 4 &minus; 9 = 12. The total number of siblings is 25 &times; 1.6 = 40, so 0 &times; 4 + 1 &times; 9 + 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 40, which gives 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 31. Subtract twice the first equation: <i>y</i> = 31 &minus; 24 = 7.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 5 is the number of students with 2 siblings (<i>x</i>), not 3.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This comes from an arithmetic slip, such as using 30 instead of 31 for 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i>.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 5 students with 2 and 7 with 3: 9 + 10 + 21 = 40 siblings, 40 &divide; 25 = 1.6.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 12 is the total number of students with 2 or 3 siblings, not those with exactly 3.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-07", "topic": "statistics", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>On a quiz, 6 students scored 70, 5 students scored 80, <i>x</i> students scored 90, and 4 students scored 100. No other scores were given. If the median score is 90, what is the least possible value of <i>x</i>?</p>", "opts": ["7", "8", "9", "11"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Listed in order, the 11 lowest scores are 70s and 80s, the next <i>x</i> are 90s, and the last 4 are 100s. There are 15 + <i>x</i> scores in all. For the median to be 90, the middle score (or both middle scores) must be a 90, so the middle position must be at least the 12th. Try <i>x</i> = 7: 22 scores, the two middle ones are the 11th and 12th, which are 80 and 90, giving a median of 85. Try <i>x</i> = 8: 23 scores, the middle one is the 12th, which is a 90. So the least value is 8.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> With <i>x</i> = 7 there are 22 scores, and the median is the average of the 11th score (80) and the 12th score (90), which is 85.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> With 23 scores the median is the 12th score, the first 90.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> <i>x</i> = 9 also gives a median of 90, but it is not the least such value; this comes from requiring an even count so that both middle scores are 90.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Matching the 11 scores below 90 with 11 scores of 90 is more than needed, because the 4 scores of 100 also sit above the 90s.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-08", "topic": "probability-counting", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>How many 3-digit positive integers have digits whose product is 12?</p>", "a": "15", "exp": "<p>No digit can be 0. Find all sets of three digits from 1 to 9 with product 12: {1, 2, 6}, {1, 3, 4}, and {2, 2, 3}. (Any set using 12 itself or a digit 5, 7, 8, or 9 fails.) Count arrangements: {1, 2, 6} gives 3! = 6 numbers, {1, 3, 4} gives 6, and {2, 2, 3} gives 3 (the 3 can be in any of the three positions). Total: 6 + 6 + 3 = 15.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-09", "topic": "probability-counting", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>A box holds 5 red cards and 3 blue cards. Three cards are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that exactly 2 of the 3 cards are red? Express your answer as a fraction in lowest terms.</p>", "a": "15/28", "exp": "<p>Count unordered sets of 3 cards: there are <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">8 &times; 7 &times; 6</span><span class=\"fden\">3 &times; 2 &times; 1</span></span> = 56 of them. Favorable sets have 2 of the 5 red cards and 1 of the 3 blue cards: 10 &times; 3 = 30. So the probability is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">30</span><span class=\"fden\">56</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">15</span><span class=\"fden\">28</span></span>.</p><p>Alternatively, by order: P(red, red, blue) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span class=\"fden\">8</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">4</span><span class=\"fden\">7</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">60</span><span class=\"fden\">336</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span class=\"fden\">28</span></span>, and the blue card can come first, second, or third, so 3 &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span class=\"fden\">28</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">15</span><span class=\"fden\">28</span></span>. A common error is to forget that factor of 3.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:probstats-10", "topic": "statistics", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A list contains 6 numbers. If the greatest number is removed, the mean of the remaining 5 numbers is 3 less than the mean of all 6. If instead the least number is removed, the mean of the remaining 5 numbers is 2 more than the mean of all 6. What is the difference between the greatest and least numbers in the list?</p>", "opts": ["1", "5", "25", "30"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let <i>m</i> be the mean of all 6 numbers, so the total is 6<i>m</i>. Removing the greatest number <i>g</i> leaves a total of 5(<i>m</i> &minus; 3) = 5<i>m</i> &minus; 15, so <i>g</i> = 6<i>m</i> &minus; (5<i>m</i> &minus; 15) = <i>m</i> + 15. Removing the least number <i>l</i> leaves 5(<i>m</i> + 2) = 5<i>m</i> + 10, so <i>l</i> = 6<i>m</i> &minus; (5<i>m</i> + 10) = <i>m</i> &minus; 10. Then <i>g</i> &minus; <i>l</i> = 25. Check with 10, 15, 18, 20, 22, 35: mean 20; without 35 the mean is 17; without 10 the mean is 22.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 3 &minus; 2 = 1 subtracts the two changes in the mean, but the changes in the mean are not the distances of the numbers from the mean.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 3 + 2 = 5 adds the changes in the mean without multiplying by the 5 remaining numbers that each change is spread over.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> The greatest number is 15 above the mean and the least is 10 below it, so they differ by 25.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> (3 + 2) &times; 6 = 30 scales by the original 6 numbers, but each change in the mean is spread over the 5 numbers that remain.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-01", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Pipe A alone can fill an empty tank in 4 hours, and pipe B alone can fill it in 6 hours. A drain at the bottom can empty the full tank in 12 hours. The tank starts empty with the drain open. Pipe A is turned on at the start, and pipe B is turned on 2 hours later. The drain stays open the whole time. How many hours after the start is the tank full?</p>", "opts": ["2", "3", "3.2", "4"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>Rates per hour: A fills <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span>, B fills <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span>, the drain empties <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span>. For the first 2 hours only A and the drain are working, so the net rate is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> per hour, and after 2 hours the tank is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> full. Once B joins, the net rate is <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> &minus; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">4</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> per hour. The remaining <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> takes <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> &divide; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = 2 more hours, so the tank is full 2 + 2 = 4 hours after the start.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 2 hours is only the time after pipe B is turned on; the first 2 hours must be added.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 3 hours is the answer if all three (A, B, and the drain) ran from the start.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 3.2 hours is the answer if the drain is ignored.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> is filled in the first 2 hours, and the remaining <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> at <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> per hour takes 2 more hours.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-02", "topic": "fractions-percents", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>The price of a bicycle was increased by 25%. The new price was then decreased by <i>x</i>%, and the final price was 10% less than the original price. What is the value of <i>x</i>?</p>", "opts": ["15", "20", "28", "35"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let the original price be 100. After the 25% increase the price is 125. The final price is 10% less than 100, which is 90. The decrease is from 125 to 90, a drop of 35 out of 125, so <i>x</i> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">35</span><span class=\"fden\">125</span></span> &times; 100 = 28.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 15 comes from subtracting the percents (25 &minus; 10), but percent changes do not add when they act on different bases.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> A 20% decrease would undo the 25% increase exactly, returning to the original price, not to 10% below it.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> The price must fall from 125 to 90, and 35 is 28% of 125.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 35 is the drop in dollars (on an original price of 100), not the percent of the increased price 125.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-03", "topic": "fractions-percents", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A container holds 20 liters of a mixture that is 30% alcohol by volume. Some of the mixture is poured out and replaced with the same volume of pure alcohol, so that the container still holds 20 liters. The new mixture is 44% alcohol. How many liters were poured out and replaced?</p>", "opts": ["2.8", "4", "5", "6"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The original mixture contains 0.30 &times; 20 = 6 liters of alcohol. Pouring out <i>x</i> liters of mixture removes 0.3<i>x</i> liters of alcohol, and adding <i>x</i> liters of pure alcohol adds <i>x</i> liters. The final mixture must contain 0.44 &times; 20 = 8.8 liters of alcohol, so 6 &minus; 0.3<i>x</i> + <i>x</i> = 8.8, giving 0.7<i>x</i> = 2.8 and <i>x</i> = 4.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 2.8 is the increase in alcohol (8.8 &minus; 6), which ignores that the poured-out liquid also contained alcohol.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Each liter replaced adds a net 0.7 liter of alcohol, and 2.8 &divide; 0.7 = 4.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 5 is the amount of pure alcohol you would add if nothing were poured out, but then the container would hold 25 liters.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 6 is the number of liters of alcohol in the original mixture, not the amount replaced.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-04", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>On a map, <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> inch represents 10 miles. A rectangular park is drawn on the map as a rectangle 1<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> inches long and 2<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> inches wide. What is the actual area of the park, in square miles?</p>", "a": "600", "exp": "<p>Since <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> inch represents 10 miles, 1 inch represents 10 &divide; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> miles. The length is 1<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = 20 miles and the width is 2<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">9</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> &times; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = 30 miles. The area is 20 &times; 30 = 600 square miles. (A common error is to find the map area, 3<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">8</span></span> square inches, and multiply by <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">40</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> only once, giving 45; area scales by the square of the scale factor.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-05", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A leaking faucet drips at a steady rate of 2 drops per second. It takes 20 drops to make 1 milliliter, and 1 liter = 1,000 milliliters. How many liters of water drip from the faucet in a 30-day month?</p>", "opts": ["8.64", "129.6", "259.2", "5,184"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>One day has 24 &times; 60 &times; 60 = 86,400 seconds, so 30 days have 2,592,000 seconds. At 2 drops per second that is 5,184,000 drops. Dividing by 20 drops per milliliter gives 259,200 milliliters, and dividing by 1,000 gives 259.2 liters.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 8.64 liters is the amount for one day, not 30 days.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 129.6 uses 1 drop per second instead of 2.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 5,184,000 drops &divide; 20 = 259,200 mL = 259.2 L.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 5,184 skips the conversion from drops to milliliters (dividing by 20).</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-06", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Working alone, Maria can paint a fence in 4 hours, and Josh can paint the same fence in 6 hours. They start painting together. After 1 hour, Maria leaves and Josh finishes the fence alone. How many minutes in total does Josh spend painting?</p>", "a": "270", "exp": "<p>In one hour Maria paints <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> of the fence and Josh paints <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span>, so together they finish <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> in the first hour. The remaining <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">7</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> is painted by Josh alone at <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> per hour, which takes <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">7</span><span class=\"fden\">12</span></span> &divide; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">6</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">7</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> = 3<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> hours. Josh paints for 1 + 3<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> = 4<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span> hours, which is 270 minutes. (Do not forget the first hour; 210 minutes is only the time Josh works alone.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-07", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>A cyclist rides from her home to a lake. For the first <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> of the distance she rides uphill at 10 miles per hour, and for the remaining <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> of the distance she rides at 40 miles per hour. What is her average speed, in miles per hour, for the entire trip?</p>", "opts": ["16", "20", "25", "30"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Choose a convenient total distance, such as 120 miles. The first 40 miles at 10 mph take 4 hours, and the last 80 miles at 40 mph take 2 hours. The whole trip is 120 miles in 6 hours, so the average speed is 120 &divide; 6 = 20 mph. (The answer does not depend on the distance chosen.)</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 16 is the average speed if half the distance were at each speed, not one third and two thirds.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Total distance divided by total time: 120 &divide; (4 + 2) = 20.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 25 is the simple average of 10 and 40, which would only be right if the two speeds were used for equal amounts of time.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 30 weights the speeds by distance (one part at 10, two parts at 40) instead of by time; more time is spent on the slow part.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-08", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>In a chess club, the ratio of the number of boys to the number of girls is 5:3. After 4 boys leave the club and 4 girls join, the ratio of boys to girls becomes 3:2. How many girls were in the club originally?</p>", "opts": ["20", "60", "64", "100"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Let the original numbers be 5<i>n</i> boys and 3<i>n</i> girls. After the change there are 5<i>n</i> &minus; 4 boys and 3<i>n</i> + 4 girls, and (5<i>n</i> &minus; 4) : (3<i>n</i> + 4) = 3 : 2. Cross-multiplying, 2(5<i>n</i> &minus; 4) = 3(3<i>n</i> + 4), so 10<i>n</i> &minus; 8 = 9<i>n</i> + 12 and <i>n</i> = 20. Originally there were 3 &times; 20 = 60 girls. Check: 100 boys and 60 girls become 96 and 64, and 96:64 = 3:2.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 20 is the value of <i>n</i>, the multiplier in the ratio, not the number of girls.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 3<i>n</i> = 60.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 64 is the number of girls after 4 joined, not the original number.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 100 is the original number of boys.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-09", "topic": "fractions-percents", "rating": 5, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>In a school, 60% of the students are girls. Of all the students in the school, 45% play a musical instrument. Of the girls, 30% play a musical instrument. What percent of the boys play a musical instrument? Express your answer as a decimal, without the percent sign.</p>", "a": "67.5", "exp": "<p>Take the school to have 200 students. Then there are 120 girls and 80 boys, and 45% of 200 = 90 students play an instrument. Of the girls, 30% of 120 = 36 play an instrument, so 90 &minus; 36 = 54 of the instrument players are boys. The percent of boys who play is 54 &divide; 80 = 0.675, which is 67.5%. (The 15% obtained by subtracting 45% &minus; 30% is not a percent of the boys; the two percents have different bases.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:rates-10", "topic": "ratios-rates", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Six identical machines, each running 8 hours a day, produce 1,200 parts in 10 days. At the same rate per machine, how many days will it take 9 of these machines, each running 5 hours a day, to produce 1,350 parts?</p>", "opts": ["7.5", "12", "18", "27"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Find the rate for one machine-hour. The six machines run 6 &times; 8 &times; 10 = 480 machine-hours to make 1,200 parts, so one machine-hour makes 1,200 &divide; 480 = 2.5 parts. Nine machines running 5 hours a day provide 45 machine-hours per day, which make 45 &times; 2.5 = 112.5 parts per day. Producing 1,350 parts takes 1,350 &divide; 112.5 = 12 days.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 7.5 ignores that each machine now runs 5 hours a day instead of 8.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 1,350 parts &divide; 112.5 parts per day = 12 days.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 18 ignores that there are now 9 machines instead of 6.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 27 scales the time up by 9/6 for more machines; more machines should make the job faster, not slower.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-01", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Lena has some $5 bills and some $1 bills worth $38 in all. If instead she had half as many $5 bills and twice as many $1 bills, her bills would be worth $31. How many bills does Lena actually have?</p>", "opts": ["6", "8", "14", "19"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let <i>f</i> be the number of $5 bills and <i>o</i> the number of $1 bills. Then 5<i>f</i> + <i>o</i> = 38 and 2.5<i>f</i> + 2<i>o</i> = 31. Doubling the second equation gives 5<i>f</i> + 4<i>o</i> = 62; subtracting the first gives 3<i>o</i> = 24, so <i>o</i> = 8 and <i>f</i> = 6. She has 6 + 8 = 14 bills.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 6 is the number of $5 bills only; the question asks for all bills.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 8 is the number of $1 bills only.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 6 five-dollar bills and 8 one-dollar bills make 14 bills.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 19 counts the bills in the imaginary situation (3 fives and 16 ones), not what she actually has.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-02", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Ana is now 3 times as old as her brother. In 8 years, Ana will be twice as old as her brother will be then. How old was Ana 4 years ago?</p>", "opts": ["8", "20", "24", "32"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Let the brother be <i>b</i> years old now, so Ana is 3<i>b</i>. In 8 years: 3<i>b</i> + 8 = 2(<i>b</i> + 8), so 3<i>b</i> + 8 = 2<i>b</i> + 16 and <i>b</i> = 8. Ana is 24 now, so 4 years ago she was 20.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 8 is the brother's age now.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Ana is 24 now and was 20 four years ago.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 24 is Ana's age now; the question asks about 4 years ago.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 32 is Ana's age 8 years from now.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-03", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Rosa drove from her home to the beach at a constant 40 miles per hour and drove home along the same road at a constant 60 miles per hour. The round trip took exactly 5 hours of driving. How many minutes longer did the drive to the beach take than the drive home?</p>", "opts": ["30", "48", "60", "120"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let <i>d</i> be the one-way distance in miles. Then <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>d</i></span><span class=\"fden\">40</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\"><i>d</i></span><span class=\"fden\">60</span></span> = 5. Multiplying by 120: 3<i>d</i> + 2<i>d</i> = 600, so <i>d</i> = 120. The drive there took 120 &divide; 40 = 3 hours; the drive home took 120 &divide; 60 = 2 hours. The difference is 1 hour = 60 minutes.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 30 comes from assuming each leg took 2.5 hours and splitting the difference; the legs are not equal in time.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 48 is the average speed for the round trip in miles per hour, not a time.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 3 hours minus 2 hours is 60 minutes.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 120 is the one-way distance in miles, not the time difference.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-04", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Each of the 32 students in a class plays soccer, basketball, both, or neither. Exactly 18 students play soccer, exactly 15 play basketball, and 6 play neither sport. How many more students play exactly one of the two sports than play both?</p>", "opts": ["7", "12", "19", "26"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Students who play at least one sport: 32 &minus; 6 = 26. Then 18 + 15 &minus; (both) = 26, so 7 play both. Exactly one sport: 26 &minus; 7 = 19. The difference is 19 &minus; 7 = 12.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 7 is the number who play both sports, one of the two quantities being compared.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> 19 play exactly one sport and 7 play both; 19 &minus; 7 = 12.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 19 is the number who play exactly one sport; the question asks how many more than the \"both\" group.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 26 is the number who play at least one sport; it does not account for the overlap.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-05", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Five different positive integers have a mean of 12 and a median of 10. What is the greatest possible value of the largest of the five integers?</p>", "opts": ["35", "36", "37", "47"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The sum is 5 &times; 12 = 60 and the middle number is 10. To make the largest as big as possible, make the other three as small as possible: the two below the median are 1 and 2, and the one just above the median must be at least 11 (the integers are different). Then the largest is 60 &minus; (1 + 2 + 10 + 11) = 36.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 35 comes from using 12 instead of 11 for the fourth number; 11 is allowed since it is larger than the median.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The set 1, 2, 10, 11, 36 has sum 60 and median 10.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 37 requires the two smallest to both be 1, but the integers must be different.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 47 forgets the fourth number entirely (60 &minus; 1 &minus; 2 &minus; 10).</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-06", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 4, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Tickets to a show cost $7 for each adult and $4 for each child. A group bought at least one adult ticket and at least one child ticket and spent exactly $100. What is the greatest possible number of people in the group?</p>", "a": "22", "exp": "<p>Let <i>a</i> be the number of adults and <i>c</i> the number of children, so 7<i>a</i> + 4<i>c</i> = 100. To have the most people, use as few of the expensive adult tickets as possible. Since 100 and 4<i>c</i> are both multiples of 4, 7<i>a</i> must be a multiple of 4, so <i>a</i> must be a multiple of 4. The smallest allowed is <i>a</i> = 4, giving 4<i>c</i> = 72 and <i>c</i> = 18. The group has 4 + 18 = 22 people. (Checking <i>a</i> = 1, 2, or 3 gives 93, 86, or 79 dollars for children, none divisible by 4.)</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-07", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 5, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Mr. Diaz is now 4 times as old as his daughter. Six years ago, he was 7 times as old as his daughter was then. In how many years from now will Mr. Diaz be exactly 3 times as old as his daughter?</p>", "a": "6", "exp": "<p>Let the daughter be <i>d</i> years old now, so Mr. Diaz is 4<i>d</i>. Six years ago: 4<i>d</i> &minus; 6 = 7(<i>d</i> &minus; 6), so 4<i>d</i> &minus; 6 = 7<i>d</i> &minus; 42 and 3<i>d</i> = 36, giving <i>d</i> = 12 and Mr. Diaz is 48. In <i>x</i> years: 48 + <i>x</i> = 3(12 + <i>x</i>), so 48 + <i>x</i> = 36 + 3<i>x</i> and <i>x</i> = 6. Check: in 6 years he is 54 and she is 18, and 54 = 3 &times; 18.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-08", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 5, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>Kai spent <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> of his money on a book, then <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> of what was left on lunch, and then $9 on a movie ticket. He then had $21 left. How much money did Kai have at the start?</p>", "opts": ["42", "60", "72", "90"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>Work backward. Before the movie he had 21 + 9 = $30. Lunch took <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> of what he had, so $30 was <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> of that amount: 30 &divide; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> = $45. The book took <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span>, so $45 was <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> of the start: 45 &divide; <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">4</span></span> = $60. Check: 60 &rarr; 45 &rarr; 30 &rarr; 21.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 42 comes from forgetting to add back the $9 for the movie before undoing the fractions (21 &rarr; 31.5 &rarr; 42).</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Starting with $60: book $15, lunch $15, movie $9, leaving $21.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> 72 comes from treating the <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">1</span><span class=\"fden\">3</span></span> as a third of the original money rather than of what remained.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 90 comes from undoing the lunch step by multiplying $30 by 3 instead of by <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span class=\"fden\">2</span></span>.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-09", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 5, "kind": "grid", "html": "<p>Towns A and B are 315 miles apart along a straight railroad. A train leaves A at 9:00 a.m. traveling toward B at a constant 50 miles per hour. A second train leaves B at 10:30 a.m. traveling toward A at a constant 70 miles per hour. How many miles from town A are the trains when they meet?</p>", "a": "175", "exp": "<p>By 10:30 a.m. the first train has traveled 1.5 &times; 50 = 75 miles, so 315 &minus; 75 = 240 miles separate the trains. After that they close the gap at 50 + 70 = 120 miles per hour, so they meet 240 &divide; 120 = 2 hours later, at 12:30 p.m. The first train has then traveled 3.5 hours &times; 50 = 175 miles from A. Check: the second train travels 2 &times; 70 = 140 miles, and 175 + 140 = 315.</p>"}, {"id": "gen:words-10", "topic": "word-problems", "rating": 4, "kind": "mc", "html": "<p>At a school, the ratio of boys to girls was 7 : 5. After 18 boys left and 6 girls joined, there were equal numbers of boys and girls. How many more boys than girls were at the school originally?</p>", "opts": ["12", "18", "24", "84"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>Let the original numbers be 7<i>x</i> boys and 5<i>x</i> girls. After the change, 7<i>x</i> &minus; 18 = 5<i>x</i> + 6, so 2<i>x</i> = 24 and <i>x</i> = 12. Originally there were 84 boys and 60 girls, so there were 84 &minus; 60 = 24 more boys.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> 12 is the value of <i>x</i>, the number of students in one \"part\" of the ratio, not the difference.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> 18 is the number of boys who left.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> 84 boys minus 60 girls is 24.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> 84 is the original number of boys, not how many more boys than girls.</p>"}];
